[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the host firewall (iptables) on the site’s default gateway, router6, specifically the forwarding policy on its WAN-facing interface eth2 (10.50.1.2) toward router3 (10.50.1.1).

Mechanism: Clients on 172.18.51.0/24 can reach the gateway (172.18.51.254) reliably, but any traffic that leaves the site toward “the internet” (via eth2) has its return packets dropped by router6’s FORWARD chain. The rules include blanket drops for any packet entering on eth2, so replies from upstream are discarded, making user sessions appear to “hiccup” or hang simultaneously for all four users.

Localization: Router6 eth1 serves the LAN (172.18.51.254/24) and eth2 connects upstream (10.50.1.2/24). Evidence:
- From a LAN host (pc16), ping to the gateway succeeds (0% loss), but ping to upstream 10.50.1.1 is 100% loss.
- The layer-2 between switch10 ↔ mswitch2 ↔ router6 is healthy (no link flaps; STP forwarding on br_vlan51).
- The L3 link between router6 and router3 is healthy (mutual pings succeed when tested on the routers).
- On router6, iptables shows:
  -P FORWARD ACCEPT
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
These drop all return traffic from upstream.

Root cause: Misconfigured firewall on router6 that unconditionally drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2.

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust the eth2 drop rules, and add a stateful allow before any drops, e.g.:
iptables -I FORWARD 1 -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
Then delete the blanket -i eth2 DROP rules or scope them properly. This will restore stable internet reachability for all LAN hosts.
=== END ===